": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_04", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_05", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_06", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_07", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "support"}], "narrative": "The window holds 2853 events over 1623 nodes with a repeat ratio of only 0.014 (step_01), i.e. an essentially non-recurrent, thinly spread log. The queried nodes are very low-degree: 2-5 events and as many distinct partners each (step_04), so each of them essentially fires once per neighbour and cannot supply a burst of closing edges. Static triangle counts are minimal \u2014 13 triangles incident to all 12 nodes together, at most 2 for any single node (step_03) \u2014 and the 2-hop neighbourhoods (step_05, step_06) show that these nodes attach mainly to hub-dominated stars (28, 1583, 1049) rather than to mutually closing triads. Where a triad does exist statically (e.g. 80-175-959, 106-236-792), the members' events are scattered across widely separated 1000-unit buckets with long empty gaps (step_07, step_08), so the putative closing interactions cannot follow their supporting interactions within 800 time units. High hub volume (step_02: node 28 with 205 events, 1583 with 129) explains the ignore-time connectivity without any prompt closure. Conclusion: the static triads are artefacts of hub attachment, not a genuine temporal closure wave."}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_1852ce27\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"closure_wave\",\n  \"entities\": [6, 10, 17, 37, 57, 72, 80, 87, 106, 111, 114, 116],\n  \"window\": [43373, 55373],\n  \"confidence\": 0.72,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"The window holds 2853 events over 1623 nodes with a repeat ratio of only 0.014 (step_01), i.e. an essentially non-recurrent, thinly spread log. The queried nodes are very low-degree: 2-5 events and as many distinct partners each (step_04), so each of them essentially fires once per neighbour and cannot supply a burst of closing edges. Static triangle counts are minimal \u2014 13 triangles incident to all 12 nodes together, at most 2 for any single node (step_03) \u2014 and the 2-hop neighbourhoods (step_05, step_06) show that these nodes attach mainly to hub-dominated stars (28, 1583, 1049) rather than to mutually closing triads. Where a triad does exist statically (e.g. 80-175-959, 106-236-792), the members' events are scattered across widely separated 1000-unit buckets with long empty gaps (step_07, step_08), so the putative closing interactions cannot follow their supporting interactions within 800 time units. High hub volume (step_02: node 28 with 205 events, 1583 with 129) explains the ignore-time connectivity without any prompt closure. Conclusion: the static triads are artefacts of hub attachment, not a genuine temporal closure wave.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 24437, "total_output_tokens": 2633, "total_tokens": 27070, "total_latency_ms": 40261, "llm_calls": 5, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}